{
  "abstract": "For a Diophantine triple {a,b,c} over a field K of characteristic 0, the extending elements are x(T)=X(T)/abc for T in the coset P0+2E(K) of the induced curve E, and regular extension is translation by S=(1,rst). DKMS sextuples use T, T+S, T-S with 3S=O. We ask which other differences Delta make x(T)x(T+Delta)+1 a square identically along the coset. Theorem 1: if sqrt(-1) is not in K, the only such Delta in 2E(K) are ±S. So a coset-identical translation family has at most three extra elements, with equality iff 3S=O, over Q, Q(t) and every number field without i. The proof reduces to the parity of div f_Delta, and its polynomial identities are certified exactly in PARI. Corollary 2: for every other Delta in 2E(K) the condition holds at only finitely many points, because it defines a double cover of E of genus at least 5. Hence a rational septuple needs at least three such sporadic conditions on every base triple with 4S≠O. For 4S=O the two residual conditions are Gibbs conditions, already treated 2-adically in rStation paper 4; an exact search on six further order-four curves finds no hits. The hypothesis on i is sharp. Over Q(i) the 2-torsion point (-ab,0) is a good difference on the curve c=(a+b+2iab)/(1+ab), and with 2S=(-ab,0) this yields Diophantine septuples over Q(i). We give 76 explicit ones, verified by two independent exact programs, and infinitely many on one fibre. The smallest is i·{9/10, 25/24, 16/15, 480704/547215, 3296631/3433960, 450450/516961, 1374025/1236696}. Invariants of all 21,780 base triples of the 1089 known rational sextuples agree with the theorem: ord S is infinite or 3, and every identical pair condition is a ±S edge. No rational septuple is constructed or excluded.",
